# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ADDED — these were previously untracked-by-accident rather than by
# design, which is how ~90MB of old checkpoint .zip files ended up
# permanently baked into git history across the "Update5/6/7/8" commits.
# None of this belongs in version control: checkpoints and videos are
# large binary training artifacts that change constantly and are already
# managed by the training scripts themselves, not by git.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
checkpoints/
mario_brain_*checkpoint*.zip
# EXCEPTION: the master checkpoint IS tracked, so a clone gets a working
# trained agent immediately instead of an untrained random policy. At 20MB
# that's a reasonable one-off cost - just don't commit it after every retrain
# (see the note above `mario_brain_*checkpoint*.zip`: committing a changed
# binary repeatedly adds a near-full new copy to git history each time,
# which is exactly how this repo got bloated before). Commit an updated
# checkpoint deliberately, when there's an actual improvement worth sharing.
!mario_brain_checkpoint.zip
logs/
.milestone_saved_*.flag

